WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.699
Orders at the fruit shop come on printed forms.

2
00:00:05.699 --> 00:00:10.178
Every form starts with the same lines: the variety, the weight, a note.

3
00:00:10.178 --> 00:00:12.891
A gift-box form adds a line for the ribbon.

4
00:00:12.891 --> 00:00:15.667
A delivery form adds a line for the address.

5
00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:20.769
A helper fills in a form one line at a time, and hands it back after each line.

6
00:00:20.769 --> 00:00:22.010
Here's the odd part.

7
00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:27.533
The helper fills in the weight on a gift-box form, and what comes back is: an order form.

8
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:30.388
The ribbon line is still printed on the paper.

9
00:00:30.388 --> 00:00:33.180
But nobody is allowed to look at it any more.

10
00:00:33.333 --> 00:00:39.431
It's like handing your birthday cake over to be boxed, and getting it back with a label that says: food.

11
00:00:39.431 --> 00:00:40.545
Not wrong, exactly.

12
00:00:40.545 --> 00:00:43.828
Just no help at all when you're looking for the candles.

13
00:00:44.733 --> 00:00:47.608
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

14
00:00:47.608 --> 00:00:50.732
The form really is a gift-box form the whole time.

15
00:00:50.732 --> 00:00:52.732
So why does the compiler forget?

16
00:00:53.800 --> 00:01:00.932
In code, the form is a builder: an object you fill in one setting at a time, and then ask to build the finished thing.

17
00:01:00.932 --> 00:01:05.767
Each setter returns the builder, so the calls can follow each other on one line.

18
00:01:05.767 --> 00:01:07.943
That style is called a fluent chain.

19
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:13.117
Put the shared setter, grams, in a base class called PlainBuilder.

20
00:01:13.117 --> 00:01:17.947
Then add a subclass, PlainGiftBoxBuilder, with a setter of its own: ribbon.

21
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:20.046
Now two chains.

22
00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.011
Ribbon first, then grams.

23
00:01:22.011 --> 00:01:24.212
Or grams first, then ribbon.

24
00:01:24.212 --> 00:01:25.706
Which one compiles?

25
00:01:25.706 --> 00:01:26.964
Pause and guess.

26
00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:31.800
Ribbon first compiles.

27
00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.328
Grams first does not.

28
00:01:33.328 --> 00:01:38.783
Kotlin says: unresolved reference ribbon, on receiver of type PlainBuilder.

29
00:01:38.783 --> 00:01:41.256
And Java says: cannot find symbol.

30
00:01:42.133 --> 00:01:44.954
Follow the types, the way the compiler does.

31
00:01:44.954 --> 00:01:50.466
Each call returns its declared type, and the compiler goes by that, not by the object.

32
00:01:50.466 --> 00:01:54.184
ribbon returns a PlainGiftBoxBuilder, so grams can follow.

33
00:01:54.184 --> 00:01:59.760
But grams returns a PlainBuilder, because that's all the base class knows about itself.

34
00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:02.253
So is the object still a gift box?

35
00:02:02.253 --> 00:02:03.959
The lesson's demo checks.

36
00:02:03.959 --> 00:02:09.757
It prints: after grams, static type PlainBuilder, run-time class PlainGiftBoxBuilder.

37
00:02:09.757 --> 00:02:11.054
The object is fine.

38
00:02:11.054 --> 00:02:14.601
The form has just forgotten what kind of form it is.

39
00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.055
What we want is a base class that can say: I return whatever subclass I really am.

40
00:02:20.933 --> 00:02:22.808
So let's go back to the paper.

41
00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:26.934
Print a blank at the top of the base form: this is a, blank, form.

42
00:02:26.934 --> 00:02:30.121
Each kind of form writes its own name in the blank.

43
00:02:30.121 --> 00:02:32.309
The gift-box form writes: gift box.

44
00:02:32.309 --> 00:02:37.372
Now, when the helper hands the form back, the blank says what kind of form it is.

45
00:02:37.533 --> 00:02:38.441
Let's map it.

46
00:02:38.441 --> 00:02:40.746
The blank is a type parameter, B.

47
00:02:40.746 --> 00:02:42.701
The base form is Builder&lt;B&gt;.

48
00:02:42.701 --> 00:02:50.244
Writing your own name in the blank is the subclass declaration: GiftBoxBuilder is a Builder&lt;GiftBoxBuilder&gt;.

49
00:02:50.244 --> 00:02:54.015
And the setter that hands the form back now returns B.

50
00:02:54.933 --> 00:02:58.953
Now fill in the blank, the same trick as the first lesson.

51
00:02:58.953 --> 00:03:03.736
For a GiftBoxBuilder, every B in the base class reads GiftBoxBuilder.

52
00:03:03.736 --> 00:03:08.172
So grams returns a GiftBoxBuilder, and ribbon is in reach again.

53
00:03:08.172 --> 00:03:12.816
The demo builds the finished box: gift box, 1200 grams, red ribbon.

54
00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:16.916
Now look at the bound itself: B : Builder&lt;B&gt;.

55
00:03:16.916 --> 00:03:20.028
The B inside points back at the B it bounds.

56
00:03:20.028 --> 00:03:23.140
Read it as: B is a builder whose own B is B.

57
00:03:23.333 --> 00:03:27.054
If that sentence made you a little dizzy, you're in good company.

58
00:03:27.054 --> 00:03:28.771
Read it like the form instead.

59
00:03:28.771 --> 00:03:33.465
The only names that fit the blank belong to forms that write their own name there.

60
00:03:34.333 --> 00:03:35.334
One more piece.

61
00:03:35.334 --> 00:03:40.004
Inside the base class, this is only known to be a Builder&lt;B&gt;, not a B.

62
00:03:40.004 --> 00:03:44.873
So grams returns self(), and each subclass writes self() as: return this.

63
00:03:44.873 --> 00:03:47.075
The compiler checks that it fits.

64
00:03:47.267 --> 00:03:51.474
The tempting shortcut is to write this as B, right in the base class.

65
00:03:51.474 --> 00:03:55.621
That's an unchecked cast, and erasure turns it into no check at all.

66
00:03:56.533 --> 00:03:57.602
So far so good.

67
00:03:57.602 --> 00:03:59.240
Now let's try to cheat.

68
00:03:59.240 --> 00:04:05.295
A class called Liar writes someone else's name in its blank: Builder&lt;GiftBoxBuilder&gt;.

69
00:04:05.295 --> 00:04:06.435
Does it compile?

70
00:04:06.435 --> 00:04:07.575
Pause and guess.

71
00:04:10.800 --> 00:04:11.544
It compiles.

72
00:04:11.544 --> 00:04:14.580
The bound only asks for the name of some builder.

73
00:04:14.580 --> 00:04:21.209
And here the form picture breaks, just as the code does: anyone can write another form's name in the blank.

74
00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:24.067
But the abstract self() is where you notice.

75
00:04:24.067 --> 00:04:29.038
The liar must hand back a GiftBoxBuilder it doesn't have, so it makes a fresh one.

76
00:04:29.038 --> 00:04:35.948
Fill in the weight on the liar, and what comes back is a GiftBoxBuilder: a new object, not the liar you filled in.

77
00:04:36.867 --> 00:04:39.363
And here's the slip that really happens.

78
00:04:39.363 --> 00:04:45.854
Someone adds a pickup form by copying the gift-box builder, and forgets to change the name in the blank.

79
00:04:45.854 --> 00:04:53.094
The abstract self() catches it at compile time: return type mismatch, expected GiftBoxBuilder, actual PickupBuilder.

80
00:04:53.094 --> 00:04:57.651
With this as B, the same slip would compile, and fail later, at the call.

81
00:04:58.533 --> 00:04:59.526
Now the names.

82
00:04:59.526 --> 00:05:04.558
A type parameter that stands for my own subclass is called a self type.

83
00:05:04.558 --> 00:05:09.946
A bound that mentions the type parameter itself is called a recursive bound.

84
00:05:09.946 --> 00:05:19.019
And you've met the shape before: T : Comparable&lt;T&gt;, a T that compares with its own kind, and Java's own Enum&lt;E extends Enum&lt;E&gt;&gt;.

85
00:05:19.933 --> 00:05:21.112
And the guardrail.

86
00:05:21.112 --> 00:05:25.827
In plain Kotlin code, the best builder is often the one you never write.

87
00:05:25.827 --> 00:05:29.953
A data class with default arguments and copy covers most cases.

88
00:05:29.953 --> 00:05:37.091
Reach for self types when Java callers need a builder, or when you extend a Java class that already uses one.

89
00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:39.958
So, why did the compiler forget?

90
00:05:39.958 --> 00:05:43.386
Because a base setter could only promise the base class.

91
00:05:43.386 --> 00:05:47.976
Give the base form a blank, and let each subclass write its own name there.

92
00:05:47.976 --> 00:05:51.403
A recursive bound lets a base class return its subclass.

93
00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:52.466
Quick check.

94
00:05:52.466 --> 00:05:56.221
Take a new GiftBoxBuilder, and call grams with 1200.

95
00:05:56.221 --> 00:05:58.532
What type does the compiler see?

96
00:06:01.733 --> 00:06:02.840
GiftBoxBuilder.

97
00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:07.709
grams returns B, and the declaration filled B with GiftBoxBuilder.

98
00:06:07.709 --> 00:06:09.775
So ribbon is still in reach.
